#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543647} :activity/id #uuid "40989a66-8646-5f19-90b9-e0ec4776e375" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050977 :topic/id #uuid "769cbed7-04cc-53d4-b34b-5d8b81d1dc47" :topic/math-academy-id 679} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33cbb00f-07b5-51f0-be9d-4a6856c47b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542778} {:answer/id #uuid "560c99d8-6dc4-559c-9c1c-50a616a7568f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542872} {:answer/id #uuid "995472d2-4a77-572a-a7b7-711efd1e590b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543039} {:answer/id #uuid "d8403a05-8be0-5213-a984-3ab18c368b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543194} {:answer/id #uuid "dd60e458-a551-5764-8eed-77a2db9e815e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542778} :answer-field/id #uuid "c39a56b0-17ca-5ba8-97f8-8c9ae43975ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "28e7f38a-8ff9-507f-8c27-7cd729b83484" :question/math-academy-id "q-79508" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $-3,\\,33,\\,-363,\\,…$\n\nII. $4,\\,52,\\,572,\\,…$\n\nIII. $-2,\\,24,\\,-312,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{33}{(-3)}=-11 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-363)}{33}=-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{52}{4}=13 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{572}{52}=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{24}{(-2)}=-12 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-312)}{24}=-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence I could be a geometric sequence."} :step/id #uuid "0218cccb-f086-56d9-b8f9-aa20f5c1b9cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27ff576a-8b4f-5191-bef3-da36f3c85be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542744} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ff7102-c223-52be-869b-375cd2da2fab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "abfcac51-46f8-5836-b296-e3921e8850cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-250665" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n-4,\\,16,\\,-64,\\,256,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe fifth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84c714b0c37c7f28cb83c59e3455755470ff6aa69da145c80ccea19200b1ad8f.png)\n\nNotice that the sequence is geometric, with a common ratio of \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{16}{(-4)}=-4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can get each term by multiplying the previous term by $-4.$ \n\nContinuing to list terms of the sequence until we get to the $5$th term, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/40/400ef822bd63631f65a4190f8367e84878c03a302c3202a112e9de2858ae4249.png)\n\nTherefore, the $5$th term is $-1\\,024.$"} :step/id #uuid "329da224-1a13-5b74-9041-4ca853e42fee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c75f4c4b-c7a0-59c5-8797-cff98d56b03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543159} :answer-field/id #uuid "a12b2c7e-438f-5baa-bede-06e74205a643" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6d790e0-5c33-5dec-949d-78146efef13a" :question/math-academy-id "q-250840" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{x}{4}$, $x$, and $64$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=\\frac{x}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=x,\\,{a}_{3}=64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{(\\frac{x}{4})}=x\\cdot \\frac{4}{x}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{64}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =\\frac{64}{x} \\\\ 4x & =64 \\\\ x & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a941842-0fd3-56b2-9964-8308aaf327cc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e1d3e6-abcb-56cf-b2d6-40a027a22288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542786} {:answer/id #uuid "567f9117-9e54-5558-81ff-a15554a23e2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542875} {:answer/id #uuid "aae1215c-ff4b-50d9-8fea-cc3646342824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543090} {:answer/id #uuid "c95d0dda-8d34-5570-b249-1ee8b36c4fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543165} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fb87dd-e1e7-56d0-aea8-fb448cd18b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542786} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0da2e57-6d30-583d-ba2b-acd1651fbc57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b85423f1-a316-59dd-932f-ac0693226903" :question/math-academy-id "q-109800" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,10,\\,-20,\\,…$\n\nIII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{(-5)}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-20)}{10}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II could be a geometric sequence."} :step/id #uuid "59f07007-7ea1-58c4-9a83-d350969d589e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f143723-8c41-5def-9acf-e95d5fa37bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542723} {:answer/id #uuid "28ecaefe-65a4-5f0b-9144-0855a5975c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542748} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9ca40a-7358-522b-995f-fa037a087917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542851} {:answer/id #uuid "7aed2b30-e60d-54fa-8d06-59669d0d72ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542966} {:answer/id #uuid "89381255-0dc6-50c2-a04d-d0c5657a7967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542723} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcf9c63-b025-5bf8-b0d4-e2ed9ac04b61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9c0d129-478a-533e-ada4-db9e83c26531" :question/math-academy-id "q-67450" :question/problem "Given that $8$, $2x$, and $x$ are the first three terms of a geometric sequence, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=8,\\,{a}_{2}=2x,\\,{a}_{3}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio of the first two terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2x}{8}=\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the common ratio of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{x}{2x}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the values for $r$ and solving for $x,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bf393cb-72f3-500f-a512-82e3760658ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543594}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542619}]}
